    "slug": "modes-through-the-windows",
    "title": "Playing It Through the Windows",
    "summary": "A vamp loops longer than any one CAGED window holds ideas for, and every window already contains the note you're aiming at.",
    "tags": ["modes", "fretboard", "ear"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "A vamp doesn't stop after one pass. It loops for as long as you keep it going, which means you're going to be playing over "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " for a lot longer than any single window holds ideas for. Stay put and you'll run out of things to say well before the loop does. The five CAGED windows solved that problem before this pathway even started — you already know how to move between them; what's new is what you're moving toward."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "progression-player",
      "props": {
        "chords": ["Am", "D", "Am", "D"],
        "bpm": 66,
        "caption": "Eight beats — long enough to leave one window, cross into the next, and come back down"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Nothing about them changes for a mode. They're the same fixed spans, anchored on the root, whichever scale happens to fill them — "
        },
        {
          "text": "Nothing New to Learn on the Neck",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-no-new-shapes"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already said so for these seven, and "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Window Stays, the Third Moves",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-window-stays-put"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " said the same thing one pathway back, about the third instead of the sixth. A form is a place on the neck. It doesn't move because the notes inside it do."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's what makes moving between them cheap, and it holds exactly: every one of the five windows already contains the note you're leaning on — at least once, and usually two or three times. The thinnest case is a single copy, right at the nut; everywhere else you'll find two or three. Wherever your hand happens to be when the vamp starts, the target is already inside the box. You don't have to relocate to find it, only to keep playing once you're through it. Ionian and Aeolian don't offer this at all, and that's what having no characteristic note actually means in practice: there's nothing in any window to aim at beyond the chord tones you already know."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Take Dorian over "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
