    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Neighbouring windows overlap, and in A Dorian a copy of the "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " sits on every seam between them. The A form and the G form share "
        },
        {
          "text": "1·2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "6·2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "; the G form and the E form share "
        },
        {
          "text": "4·4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "; the E form and the D form share "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "; the D form and the C form share "
        },
        {
          "text": "5·9",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Four joins, a "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " on every one — the note you're crossing on is the same note you were aiming at. That's true of this particular vamp; it isn't a promise every mode keeps."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "text": "Get the loop going first, and keep it going — nothing here will hold a vamp under you for as long as you want it, so either play both chords yourself, or hold the root on "
          },
          {
            "text": "Drone",
            "link": {
              "kind": "screen",
              "href": "/drone"
            }
          },
          {
            "text": " and hear the "
          },
          {
            "text": "D",
            "marks": ["code"]
          },
          {
            "text": " in your head against it. A slow click from "
          },
          {
            "text": "Metronome",
            "link": {
              "kind": "screen",
              "href": "/metronome"
            }
          },
          {
            "text": " keeps the crossing honest."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "Start in whichever window your hand is already sitting in, and stay there until you've run out of ideas — not frets."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "Cross into the next window on the "
          },
          {
            "text": "F#",
            "marks": [
              "code",
              {
                "type": "color",
                "tone": "amber"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": ", not on the root."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "text": "Come back down the same way you went up."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Scale Visualizer",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/scale-visualizer"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " pages through all five windows for any mode you set it to — root "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", scale Dorian, form after form. There's no drone on that screen; it plucks a note rather than holding it. For the held root, that's still "
        },
        {
          "text": "Drone",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/drone"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Run the same routine over the Lydian, Mixolydian or Phrygian vamp and nothing about it changes — only the note and the second chord do. The frame stays the same across the whole pathway; only what fills it moves. The note is under your finger now, no matter where your hand starts. What you actually do with it once it's there is the rest of the story."
        }
      ]
    }
